1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,480
Magnificent monuments, impregnable strongholds, besieged in battle from

2
00:00:07,481 --> 00:00:15,320
Asara to missile silos. Now, forts on Modern Moders.

3
00:00:15,321 --> 00:00:29,321
This is a concertainer, a modern-day military field fortification.

4
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:37,660
It can be assembled on the battlefield in several hours.

5
00:00:37,661 --> 00:00:46,120
The units are made of heavily galvanized wire mesh, lined with polypropylene geotextile

6
00:00:46,121 --> 00:00:51,560
and filled with any available material, from sand to rocks to snow.

7
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:58,860
The concertainer was first used in the Gulf War as a British field fortification.

8
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:03,680
The densely packed walls are designed to withstand direct fire attack

9
00:01:03,681 --> 00:01:10,120
and offer blast protection from air-delivered bombs and large-caliber munitions.

10
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:17,400
The concertainer represents the culmination of an art that has evolved over thousands of years.

11
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:21,440
It is the fortified castle of the new millennium.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,880
You often hear people talking about impregnable fortresses.

13
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:26,720
It's a myth.

14
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:29,440
There is no such thing as an impregnable fortress.

15
00:01:29,441 --> 00:01:34,900
Any fortification can be taken if the attacker is willing to pay the cost.

16
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:39,840
And that's a cost in lives, a cost in equipment, and a cost in time.

17
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:45,620
The whole object of fortifications is to make that cost higher.

18
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:51,680
Fortifications are ubiquitous throughout every society in recorded history.

19
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:55,980
The earliest ones were very simply designed hill forts.

20
00:01:55,981 --> 00:02:02,860
Prehistoric man would put a crude fence of branches or bits of wood around his animals

21
00:02:02,861 --> 00:02:06,740
to form a stockade.

22
00:02:06,741 --> 00:02:11,960
Usually these forts would be situated on a hill to make it more difficult for an intruder

23
00:02:11,961 --> 00:02:14,940
to arrive unnoticed.

24
00:02:14,941 --> 00:02:19,940
But while the walls were deterrent, they were not strong enough to hold off any determined

25
00:02:19,941 --> 00:02:20,940
attacker.

26
00:02:20,941 --> 00:02:25,020
Wooden stockades are fairly fragile structures.

27
00:02:25,021 --> 00:02:26,720
You can knock them down fairly easily.

28
00:02:26,721 --> 00:02:28,280
They rot.

29
00:02:28,281 --> 00:02:29,120
They catch fire.

30
00:02:29,121 --> 00:02:30,960
All sorts of bad things happen.

31
00:02:30,961 --> 00:02:34,380
So where it was available,

32
00:02:34,381 --> 00:02:36,980
people worked in to start building walls out of stone.

33
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,780
Throughout history, many important events

34
00:02:40,781 --> 00:02:43,480
have taken place around a fortress.

35
00:02:43,481 --> 00:02:47,200
The first evidence of a city protected with stone walls

36
00:02:47,201 --> 00:02:49,060
was found in the near east.

37
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:52,500
The city of Jericho controlled that area

38
00:02:52,501 --> 00:02:55,900
because of its strategic location along the Jordan River.

39
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,720
The original walls of this desert oasis

40
00:02:59,721 --> 00:03:02,700
are believed to date back to 8,000 BC,

41
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:08,360
built by a people known as the pre-pottery Neolithic Bee.

42
00:03:08,361 --> 00:03:10,880
These people were masters of architecture.

43
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:14,260
To keep the city safe from attack,

44
00:03:14,261 --> 00:03:18,880
the 3,000 inhabitants built free-standing large stone walls

45
00:03:18,881 --> 00:03:22,680
that were six feet thick and 23 feet high.

46
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:26,980
There was a tower in one of the walls and a stairway.

47
00:03:26,981 --> 00:03:29,620
They also managed to dig a 10 foot deep ditch

48
00:03:29,621 --> 00:03:32,280
to surround the half mile circumference.

49
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:37,560
The walls of Jericho were rebuilt at least 19 times

50
00:03:37,561 --> 00:03:39,520
in the next 6,000 years

51
00:03:39,521 --> 00:03:42,000
because of damage by earthquake or war.

52
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:45,180
The famed Bible story picks up

53
00:03:45,181 --> 00:03:48,280
at the beginning of the year 1300 BC

54
00:03:48,281 --> 00:03:50,640
when the Israelites entered Palestine

55
00:03:50,641 --> 00:03:53,480
and sent Joshua to claim Jericho.

56
00:03:53,481 --> 00:03:57,560
For six days, Joshua walked around the perimeter of Jericho

57
00:03:57,561 --> 00:03:59,320
with trumpets blaring.

58
00:03:59,321 --> 00:04:01,720
And on the seventh day, as the saying goes,

59
00:04:01,721 --> 00:04:03,860
the walls came tumbling down.

60
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:06,900
The construction of stone fortresses

61
00:04:06,901 --> 00:04:10,200
became more sophisticated throughout the ancient world.

62
00:04:10,201 --> 00:04:13,440
And so did the methods for breaching those walls.

63
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:16,300
They'd had battering rams,

64
00:04:16,301 --> 00:04:18,200
They had movable towers that they could move up

65
00:04:18,201 --> 00:04:20,060
against the walls of the forts

66
00:04:20,061 --> 00:04:22,460
to allow the attacker to get over the top of the walls.

67
00:04:22,461 --> 00:04:24,380
So again, we see this constant struggle

68
00:04:24,381 --> 00:04:27,220
between the defender and the attacker.

69
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:30,340
The city of Troy in Anatolia

70
00:04:30,341 --> 00:04:33,680
was a strategically located city in the Near East

71
00:04:33,681 --> 00:04:36,820
that guarded the entrance to the Black Sea.

72
00:04:36,821 --> 00:04:40,120
The Greeks were unable to penetrate the walls.

73
00:04:40,121 --> 00:04:43,340
So in 1184 BC, they devised another method

74
00:04:43,341 --> 00:04:44,940
to conquer the fortress.

75
00:04:44,941 --> 00:04:48,700
The Greeks did not have very sophisticated siege equipment.

76
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:52,280
They just had standard personal weapons,

77
00:04:52,480 --> 00:04:54,720
so all they could try and do was climb over the walls.

78
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:57,120
And that's a hard thing to do

79
00:04:57,121 --> 00:05:00,220
against any well-built fortification

80
00:05:00,221 --> 00:05:01,360
that has a good garrison.

81
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:05,100
According to the Greek writer Homer in the Iliad,

82
00:05:05,101 --> 00:05:08,480
the city of Troy had steep 20-foot-high walls.

83
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:11,360
It was believed to be impregnable.

84
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:13,760
But the Greek general Ulysses

85
00:05:13,761 --> 00:05:18,480
Ulysses hid his men in a huge wooden horse that was offered to the city as a gift.

86
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:23,060
The gift was accepted, and the Greeks conquered Troy.

87
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:29,440
As Ulysses proved, no fortress is absolutely impregnable.

88
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:35,260
This was the cold reality that faced Herod, the king of Judea, in 35 BC.

89
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:38,560
Herod was aligned with the Roman Empire,

90
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:42,540
but he began to fear that Cleopatra coveted Masada,

91
00:05:42,541 --> 00:05:45,480
the gateway to Egypt and Africa.

92
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:52,140
The name Masada comes from the Aramaic word mitsad, which means fortress.

93
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:57,120
Herod was known as a master builder of his time,

94
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:02,220
and Masada would be the jewel in his prestigious crown of personal strongholds.

95
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:06,740
Masada's location, rising 1,300 feet above the Jordan River,

96
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:09,220
made it seemingly unapproachable.

97
00:06:09,221 --> 00:06:13,840
But keeping a supply of food and water atop the fortress was a problem.

98
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:18,160
So Herod built large, well-stocked storage houses

99
00:06:18,161 --> 00:06:22,460
and a series of cisterns which would gather the water from rain showers.

100
00:06:23,740 --> 00:06:27,120
During Herod's residence, no battles took place at Masada.

101
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:29,960
But later, in the year 66 AD,

102
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:35,020
a small group of Jewish zealots claimed Masada as their stronghold.

103
00:06:35,021 --> 00:06:42,260
It was the last bastion against the Roman army, who came back to conquer it in the year

104
00:06:42,261 --> 00:06:42,961
72.

105
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:49,540
The Romans built a camp below Masada and encircled the entire cliff fortress so no one could get

106
00:06:49,541 --> 00:06:50,241
out.

107
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:56,860
The zealots, led by Alizar Ben Yat-Yer, took advantage of two natural rock ramps leading

108
00:06:56,861 --> 00:06:59,400
to the fortress to prepare for the attack.

109
00:06:59,401 --> 00:07:08,540
They placed 100-pound rolling stones by each of these ramps or paths so that if anybody

110
00:07:08,541 --> 00:07:11,660
tried to climb up, they'd roll the stones down on them and they wouldn't be able to

111
00:07:11,661 --> 00:07:12,361
get up.

112
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:16,220
The Romans were determined to unseat the zealots from their stronghold.

113
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:22,900
After six months of preparations, Flavius Silva led his men in an attack against the

114
00:07:22,901 --> 00:07:23,601
fortress.

115
00:07:23,602 --> 00:07:27,520
And then he brought up what's known as a siege machine,

116
00:07:27,521 --> 00:07:32,240
which is a iron-plated wooden structure

117
00:07:32,241 --> 00:07:37,320
from within which archers could fire into Masada,

118
00:07:37,540 --> 00:07:38,260
the walled fortress.

119
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:40,720
And while that was going on,

120
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:43,020
the way that they covered themselves were with catapults,

121
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:46,300
and hundreds of grapefruit-sized stones

122
00:07:46,301 --> 00:07:48,020
were found on the top of Masada.

123
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:51,360
The Romans started shooting flaming arrows,

124
00:07:51,361 --> 00:07:55,840
which ultimately set fire to the inner wooden walls of the fortress.

125
00:07:56,580 --> 00:08:00,680
This built doom for the 967 zealots on Masada.

126
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:05,780
Eleazar Ben-Yair brought together the zealots and said,

127
00:08:05,781 --> 00:08:11,420
God has shown us that the tide has fatally turned against us,

128
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:16,020
but we're not going to become slaves to the Romans.

129
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:20,000
We got into this fight because we serve no one but God.

130
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:25,640
Therefore, we will only allow our deaths to be by the hand of God.

131
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:28,760
What he meant by this was that they would commit suicide.

132
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:32,660
According to the Jewish historian Josephus,

133
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:36,980
the men killed the women and children first, and then themselves.

134
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,320
With the Israelites completely in their control,

135
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:44,360
the Romans continued to expand and contain their empire

136
00:08:44,361 --> 00:08:48,620
through the use of precise, strong fortification techniques

137
00:08:48,621 --> 00:08:50,700
and sophisticated siege methods.

138
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:58,360
Their status as a dominant world power would last for several hundred years.

139
00:08:58,361 --> 00:09:06,360
The ruins of Masada were first discovered in the 1960s by a group of Israeli students traveling in the desert.

140
00:09:06,361 --> 00:09:09,360
Modern marvels will return in a moment.

141
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:19,360
In the year 120 AD, the Emperor Hadrian ordered the building of a series of walls and forts

142
00:09:19,361 --> 00:09:22,060
and forts to divide Rome and Britain

143
00:09:22,061 --> 00:09:24,300
from the unconquered Northern Territory.

144
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:30,340
It was built as much to keep the Britons in

145
00:09:30,341 --> 00:09:32,300
as to keep invaders out.

146
00:09:32,301 --> 00:09:34,840
Hadrian's Wall is a quite sophisticated fortification.

147
00:09:34,841 --> 00:09:35,840
It's a stone wall.

148
00:09:35,841 --> 00:09:37,440
It's approximately 20 feet high.

149
00:09:37,441 --> 00:09:40,180
Runs the entire width of Britain from the North Sea

150
00:09:40,181 --> 00:09:41,380
to the Irish Sea.

151
00:09:41,381 --> 00:09:44,360
There are every mile things called mile castles, which

152
00:09:44,361 --> 00:09:47,980
were small posts occupied by a full-time garrison.

153
00:09:47,981 --> 00:09:49,480
And between these were lookout towers.

154
00:09:49,481 --> 00:09:51,820
And then periodically spaced wider out

155
00:09:51,821 --> 00:09:55,980
were large fortified camps, all permanent fortifications,

156
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:57,200
built the last.

157
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:01,540
But Hadrian's Wall did not prove invincible in its day.

158
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:06,120
It was besieged by attackers outside of Roman control.

159
00:10:06,620 --> 00:10:10,020
And this, coupled with weakened imperial authority in Rome,

160
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:13,420
contributed to the end of the great Roman Empire.

161
00:10:13,421 --> 00:10:18,660
Well, like every empire, every world power,

162
00:10:18,661 --> 00:10:23,520
they basically weakened over time for a variety of reasons.

163
00:10:23,521 --> 00:10:28,200
Corruption in the government, lack of energy, as it were,

164
00:10:28,201 --> 00:10:29,540
on the part of the population.

165
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:36,160
The fall of Rome in 400 A.D. brought about the Dark Ages,

166
00:10:36,700 --> 00:10:39,820
and the art of fortification all but disappeared.

167
00:10:39,821 --> 00:10:46,820
They really went back to what was constructed back by primitive civilizations.

168
00:10:46,821 --> 00:10:51,820
Wooden stockades around towns, castles with wooden stockades around them.

169
00:10:51,821 --> 00:10:57,820
And gradually, those evolved into the castles of the Middle Ages.

170
00:10:57,821 --> 00:11:03,820
In 1066, William of Normandy crossed the English Channel from France

171
00:11:03,821 --> 00:11:06,820
and quickly overtook Britain's defenses.

172
00:11:06,821 --> 00:11:11,820
To secure his position, he began building a chain of castles.

173
00:11:11,821 --> 00:11:15,820
These castles were populated by individual lords and barons

174
00:11:15,821 --> 00:11:18,820
who ruled their immediate surrounding territories.

175
00:11:18,821 --> 00:11:22,820
The number of Norman castles grew into the hundreds.

176
00:11:22,821 --> 00:11:25,820
Typically, at that period, there was one nobleman

177
00:11:25,821 --> 00:11:27,820
who would attack another one.

178
00:11:27,821 --> 00:11:32,820
It was small actions, a very lawless period on the whole,

179
00:11:32,821 --> 00:11:34,820
very little central government.

180
00:11:34,821 --> 00:11:39,700
Over the next 200 years, a more stable, centralized society developed,

181
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:44,700
and the techniques of castle building and the purpose of the castle matured as well.

182
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:51,700
In the British Isles, they became known as the bones of the kingdom, the king's fortresses.

183
00:11:51,701 --> 00:11:58,920
During the Middle Ages, the castle evolved as a preeminent example of a defensive structure

184
00:11:58,921 --> 00:12:00,800
that could be used as a pivot in warfare.

185
00:12:00,801 --> 00:12:06,860
The castles were constructed of quarried stone and bound with a mortar made of sand and lime.

186
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:09,600
Hundreds of craftsmen worked on the structures.

187
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:16,980
The elaborate designs included an outer curtain wall, which was maculated or indented with merlins,

188
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:21,380
to which the defenders could fire their weapons and then duck for cover.

189
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:30,100
The castles were equipped with protruding towers at the corners so that all sides of the curtain wall could be covered.

190
00:12:30,101 --> 00:12:35,280
and at certain points along the wall were mertrier, or murder holes,

191
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:40,320
through which the defenders could drop lethal objects like boiling oil upon the attackers

192
00:12:40,321 --> 00:12:42,720
if they succeeded in getting through the gate.

193
00:12:44,700 --> 00:12:49,140
In the 13th century, Edward I built many castles in Wales

194
00:12:49,141 --> 00:12:51,720
to protect England's interests there.

195
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:56,080
And you particularly begin to see that in the 12th and the 13th century,

196
00:12:56,220 --> 00:12:59,220
castles become much more sophisticated, much more powerful.

197
00:12:59,221 --> 00:13:06,060
You also see towns beginning to develop, and towns become more important.

198
00:13:06,061 --> 00:13:11,480
And because it's still not a very settled time, you needed fortifications to protect those towns.

199
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:16,100
Of course, the classic example of that is Carcassonne in the south of France.

200
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:24,880
It's a perfect example of a fortress or fortified town of the Middle Ages.

201
00:13:25,240 --> 00:13:28,360
The world has become a walled fortress.

202
00:13:28,361 --> 00:13:36,580
The Great Wall of China, which was begun in 214 BC, covered 1,400 miles of mountainous terrain.

203
00:13:37,580 --> 00:13:43,240
The wall was 40 feet high in places and had 12-foot towers every 200 paces.

204
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:49,220
Built to keep out foreign invaders, it was finally abandoned in 1368.

205
00:13:50,340 --> 00:13:56,980
In 324, Constantinople became the new Rome, the seat of the Holy Roman Empire.

206
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:04,780
This great walled city stood as the center of Middle Eastern civilization for more than a thousand years,

207
00:14:05,740 --> 00:14:08,840
until it was conquered by the Turks in 1453.

208
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:16,360
It was a new technological advance that signaled the downfall of the mighty fortress,

209
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:18,740
the invention of gunpowder.

210
00:14:19,180 --> 00:14:21,340
Essentially, gunpowder made the castle obsolete.

211
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:24,140
It took a long time to reduce a castle by siege.

212
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:26,580
When gunpowder came along, this was all changed.

213
00:14:26,581 --> 00:14:30,700
an adversary could put in place a cannon

214
00:14:30,701 --> 00:14:33,780
and knock down a wall in a matter of days rather than taking months.

215
00:14:34,480 --> 00:14:37,780
Charles VIII of France introduced wheeled artillery

216
00:14:37,781 --> 00:14:41,400
that was made of a lighter weight and had higher muzzle velocity.

217
00:14:42,460 --> 00:14:45,960
Cannon fire posed a major problem for castle defenses.

218
00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:50,640
The greater the height of the walls, the better the target for the attack.

219
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:55,240
Fiery missiles would crack the stone walls, weakening them

220
00:14:55,241 --> 00:14:57,820
to the point where they would eventually fall.

221
00:14:58,900 --> 00:15:02,060
Privately owned fortresses were soon to be a thing of the past,

222
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:05,540
and the design and construction of fortifications

223
00:15:05,541 --> 00:15:09,380
became the sole domain of military engineers.

224
00:15:10,780 --> 00:15:13,460
And the main thing, the main approach that was taken

225
00:15:13,461 --> 00:15:17,320
was to lower the walls, to sink the walls into a ditch.

226
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:21,860
By doing that, you put the outer edge of the ditch

227
00:15:21,861 --> 00:15:24,400
between you and the artillery, you cannot be hit directly.

228
00:15:24,401 --> 00:15:27,760
so you're much safer from gunpowder artillery.

229
00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:30,680
So that was the basic change that everyone made.

230
00:15:31,260 --> 00:15:38,100
In the 1600s, Sébastien de Vaubin was known as a master of siege craft in the French army.

231
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:42,680
He perfected a method of attack called sap and parallel.

232
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:48,740
Attackers would dig trenches in a zigzag method up to the walls of a fortification

233
00:15:48,741 --> 00:15:54,680
to protect themselves and enable their cannons to hit their targets from a closer vantage point.

234
00:15:56,160 --> 00:16:01,800
During peacetime, Vauban was commissioned to improve existing French fortifications

235
00:16:01,801 --> 00:16:05,180
and design new impregnable strongholds.

236
00:16:07,060 --> 00:16:13,840
In the course of his military career, Vauban worked on more than 160 fort constructions.

237
00:16:13,841 --> 00:16:18,120
The fortresses built from scratch, built in open terrain,

238
00:16:18,121 --> 00:16:24,320
were basically star-shaped, and they could have anywhere from seven or eight bastions.

239
00:16:24,660 --> 00:16:30,900
And they were built symmetrically because you wanted every point of a fortification to be equally strong.

240
00:16:31,620 --> 00:16:36,040
The Vaubanian star-shaped fortress with its multiple vantage points

241
00:16:36,041 --> 00:16:41,140
enabled the defenders of the fort to cover all angles of possible attack.

242
00:16:41,141 --> 00:16:51,460
Fauban died in 1707, but his style of fortifications based on geometric and scientific calculations

243
00:16:51,461 --> 00:16:58,420
would continue to be used throughout Europe and across the ocean in the New World.

244
00:16:58,421 --> 00:17:03,280
The word dungeon is derived from the Latin words for lord and house.

245
00:17:03,281 --> 00:17:08,020
It later came to mean a room beneath the Lord's Manor, the place where the prisoners were

246
00:17:08,021 --> 00:17:09,540
kept.

247
00:17:09,541 --> 00:17:12,476
Marvels will return in a moment.

248
00:17:12,477 --> 00:17:18,440
The Vaubanian star-shaped fortress that had worked so effectively

249
00:17:18,441 --> 00:17:24,263
in Europe migrated to North America with the Spanish and French settlers.

250
00:17:24,264 --> 00:17:25,380
This elaborate

251
00:17:25,381 --> 00:17:32,860
design was also used in 1755 in the construction of Fort Ticonderoga in the state of New York on

252
00:17:32,861 --> 00:17:39,762
Lake Champlain. Built by the French, it played a role in the French and Indian War.

253
00:17:39,763 --> 00:17:40,760
It was taken

254
00:17:40,761 --> 00:17:44,990
by Ethan Allen and Benedict Arnold during the American Revolution.

255
00:17:44,991 --> 00:17:46,720
The Americans held the fort

256
00:17:46,721 --> 00:17:53,370
until July of 1777, when General Burgoyne and his British redcoats took it back.

257
00:17:53,371 --> 00:17:54,700
With Ticonderoga

258
00:17:54,701 --> 00:18:01,100
defeated, George Washington decided to fortify another strategic location, West Point.

259
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:06,520
West Point presided over a vital section of the Hudson River,

260
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:09,680
a highway for military movement at the time.

261
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:12,320
If the British gained control of the Hudson,

262
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:16,500
they would cut off a major supply source for the Continental Army.

263
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:21,980
It was essential to get men and materiel between the New England colonies

264
00:18:21,981 --> 00:18:24,300
and those to the Mid-Atlantic colonies in the South.

265
00:18:24,660 --> 00:18:27,360
Whatever industrial capacity we had was in New England.

266
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:29,000
The grain was in the West.

267
00:18:29,100 --> 00:18:32,400
It was essential that those two segments of the population

268
00:18:32,401 --> 00:18:34,600
could be brought to bear to defend the country.

269
00:18:35,340 --> 00:18:38,580
West Point, New York, is made up of rocky promontories

270
00:18:38,581 --> 00:18:42,800
on one side of the river and Constitution Island on the other.

271
00:18:43,560 --> 00:18:46,120
The river makes an S-shaped turn at this juncture,

272
00:18:46,260 --> 00:18:49,720
and that became another crucial reason for fortifying the area.

273
00:18:50,920 --> 00:18:53,780
What this meant was that any British ship coming north

274
00:18:53,781 --> 00:18:57,840
would have to slow down considerably going through the S-turn.

275
00:18:58,280 --> 00:19:00,740
And the river also was narrow at this point,

276
00:19:00,741 --> 00:19:02,800
only 400 yards wide.

277
00:19:02,980 --> 00:19:06,140
That meant that the entire river could be covered with fire

278
00:19:06,141 --> 00:19:09,480
at a point where the British ships would be going the slowest.

279
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:14,900
The Continental Congress voted to fortify these highlands in 1775.

280
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:19,000
The first man they chose to design the structures

281
00:19:19,001 --> 00:19:22,060
was a Dutch botanist named Bernard Romans.

282
00:19:22,880 --> 00:19:27,560
The selection of Bernard Romans as the first engineer at West Point

283
00:19:27,561 --> 00:19:32,160
in some ways highlights the absence of trained professional officers.

284
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:36,680
The Continental Congress did not have anyone to turn to

285
00:19:36,681 --> 00:19:40,220
to entrust with this very large project

286
00:19:40,221 --> 00:19:45,040
and were forced to turn to basically the first individual who said he could do it.

287
00:19:45,840 --> 00:19:50,280
Roman's plan for West Point became a costly and ill-fated endeavor.

288
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:54,280
And because he had no military experience,

289
00:19:54,281 --> 00:20:00,980
He didn't understand the difference between simple principles such as grazing fire and plunging fire,

290
00:20:01,180 --> 00:20:04,040
and he placed his cannons high up on the hill,

291
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:11,200
where they would actually have a very hard time aiming down and shooting at the British warships.

292
00:20:11,780 --> 00:20:15,900
Roman's batteries were abandoned in October 1777,

293
00:20:16,580 --> 00:20:21,180
but General Washington did not give up on his plan to fortify West Point.

294
00:20:21,181 --> 00:20:24,420
He needed land protection for the great chain

295
00:20:24,421 --> 00:20:27,180
that he installed across the Hudson.

296
00:20:27,181 --> 00:20:31,180
The chain was strong enough to hold the British vessels back

297
00:20:31,181 --> 00:20:34,180
as they made their way up the river.

298
00:20:34,181 --> 00:20:37,180
There were 500 links of the chain.

299
00:20:37,181 --> 00:20:40,180
Each one of those links weighs about 120 pounds.

300
00:20:40,181 --> 00:20:44,180
The total weight of the entire contraption of those links

301
00:20:44,181 --> 00:20:49,180
of the chains, as well as clevices and other stabilizing devices,

302
00:20:49,181 --> 00:20:52,180
and mobilizing devices is estimated to have weighed

303
00:20:52,181 --> 00:20:55,180
in excess of 150 tons.

304
00:20:55,181 --> 00:20:58,180
And you can imagine when you're just dealing with oxen and horses

305
00:20:58,181 --> 00:21:01,180
and men, what kind of job that was.

306
00:21:01,181 --> 00:21:06,180
In early 1778, Washington brought Thaddeus Kaczewsko,

307
00:21:06,181 --> 00:21:10,180
a Polish military engineer, to West Point

308
00:21:10,181 --> 00:21:14,180
to begin a second phase of fortifications.

309
00:21:14,181 --> 00:21:17,180
He looked at the rocky lay of the land and came up with the idea

310
00:21:17,181 --> 00:21:20,940
of building field fortifications in key locations,

311
00:21:20,941 --> 00:21:24,180
and many forts called redoubts along the river.

312
00:21:26,180 --> 00:21:28,820
This became Fortress West Point.

313
00:21:30,180 --> 00:21:34,000
A Fortress West Point consists of several forts,

314
00:21:34,001 --> 00:21:37,000
a number of redoubts on both sides of the river,

315
00:21:37,001 --> 00:21:39,780
and taking advantage of the difference in elevations.

316
00:21:39,781 --> 00:21:41,200
Kosciuszko was a master

317
00:21:41,201 --> 00:21:44,120
in utilizing the differences in terrain.

318
00:21:44,121 --> 00:21:45,580
Washington felt confident

319
00:21:45,581 --> 00:21:48,620
that the fortress at West Point would buy him time

320
00:21:48,621 --> 00:21:50,020
in the Hudson River area.

321
00:21:51,720 --> 00:21:54,620
But what he didn't count on was that his commandant

322
00:21:54,621 --> 00:21:56,120
would try to betray him.

323
00:21:57,600 --> 00:22:00,020
The closest that the British came to taking

324
00:22:00,021 --> 00:22:03,540
Fortress West Point was during the commandant ship

325
00:22:03,541 --> 00:22:04,860
of Benedict Arnold.

326
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:09,200
Benedict Arnold was becoming disillusioned

327
00:22:09,201 --> 00:22:11,480
with the revolution and saw no way

328
00:22:11,481 --> 00:22:13,840
to achieve absolute victory.

329
00:22:13,841 --> 00:22:18,180
At West Point, he began making plans to provide the British with military secrets.

330
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:22,180
He passed the information to John Andre, a British go-between.

331
00:22:23,840 --> 00:22:27,720
He began to let the fort and redoubts begin to decay,

332
00:22:28,060 --> 00:22:30,820
not keeping them up so it would make it easier for the British to attack.

333
00:22:31,620 --> 00:22:35,320
And if he had been successful, if the plans had gotten to the British,

334
00:22:35,321 --> 00:22:39,220
there's no doubt with a traitor in command of West Point,

335
00:22:39,440 --> 00:22:40,740
the British could have been successful.

336
00:22:41,300 --> 00:22:43,400
But the British never did get the plans.

337
00:22:43,840 --> 00:22:47,640
Major Andre was apprehended before delivery,

338
00:22:47,740 --> 00:22:50,140
and Benedict Arnold was accused of treason.

339
00:22:52,440 --> 00:22:55,420
West Point was never attacked by the British,

340
00:22:55,520 --> 00:22:58,920
due in part to the new interdependent system of forts

341
00:22:59,020 --> 00:23:02,460
devised by Thaddeus Kosciuszko.

342
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:07,260
This may have been simply an ad hoc series of solutions

343
00:23:07,360 --> 00:23:11,960
to a series of problems and not a grandiose scheme

344
00:23:11,961 --> 00:23:16,900
to actually come up with a revolutionary new system of fortifications.

345
00:23:17,460 --> 00:23:21,400
This is reinforced by the fact that after the American Revolution,

346
00:23:21,401 --> 00:23:26,660
you see a decline in the use of a series of fortifications

347
00:23:26,661 --> 00:23:32,200
and the return to the idea of one main fortification,

348
00:23:32,380 --> 00:23:36,560
which would lead to the building of Fort McHenry.

349
00:23:37,080 --> 00:23:41,460
Fort McHenry was a coastal fort built to defend the city of Baltimore

350
00:23:41,461 --> 00:23:46,460
against a British sea attack during the War of 1812.

351
00:23:46,461 --> 00:23:50,460
It was here that Francis Scott Key wrote the Star Spangled Banner.

352
00:23:53,460 --> 00:23:57,460
The United States continued to fortify the eastern seaboard,

353
00:23:57,461 --> 00:24:01,460
and in 1829, construction began on another coastal stronghold

354
00:24:01,461 --> 00:24:06,460
on Coxborough Island in Savannah, Georgia.

355
00:24:06,461 --> 00:24:09,460
Like Fort McHenry, the purpose of Fort Pulaski

356
00:24:09,461 --> 00:24:13,900
was to defend the Port of Savannah against foreign invaders.

357
00:24:15,800 --> 00:24:18,420
One of the original engineers on the project

358
00:24:18,421 --> 00:24:22,480
was a recent West Point graduate named Robert E. Lee.

359
00:24:23,540 --> 00:24:26,860
It was in this environment, because of its strategic location

360
00:24:26,861 --> 00:24:28,720
in the middle of the Savannah River,

361
00:24:29,180 --> 00:24:32,900
that Lee and the administration of President Madison

362
00:24:32,901 --> 00:24:34,860
decided that they should build a fort here

363
00:24:34,861 --> 00:24:37,680
that could best protect the port city of Savannah,

364
00:24:37,681 --> 00:24:40,940
protecting not only the city but of course all the commerce.

365
00:24:41,500 --> 00:24:44,400
The rice that was cultivated along the coastal areas,

366
00:24:44,700 --> 00:24:48,100
the cotton, the pitch, the tar, the pine,

367
00:24:48,300 --> 00:24:50,440
all these things were very important commodities.

368
00:24:51,200 --> 00:24:54,200
Because of Coxsburgh Island's marshy grasslands,

369
00:24:54,740 --> 00:24:57,360
Fort Pulaski had to have a special foundation

370
00:24:57,361 --> 00:24:59,460
to support its enormous weight.

371
00:25:00,540 --> 00:25:04,180
It sits on pilings that go 81 feet into the ground

372
00:25:04,181 --> 00:25:07,080
and on type of the pilings are what's called grillage.

373
00:25:07,081 --> 00:25:11,420
Grilledge is nothing more than a fancy word for a large platform.

374
00:25:11,960 --> 00:25:15,680
And on that platform, on those pilings, sit this fort.

375
00:25:16,600 --> 00:25:19,480
Fort Pulaski is pentagonal shaped.

376
00:25:20,340 --> 00:25:22,840
Its walls are seven and a half feet thick,

377
00:25:23,560 --> 00:25:28,840
constructed out of 25 million bricks that were made by local slaves.

378
00:25:29,380 --> 00:25:31,760
The fort took 18 years to build.

379
00:25:32,420 --> 00:25:36,120
An entire town of workers grew up around the construction site.

380
00:25:36,121 --> 00:25:41,006
When it was finished in 1847, U.S. Chief Engineer Joseph G.

381
00:25:41,007 --> 00:25:47,300
Totten declared that it was as invincible and strong as the Rocky Mountains.

382
00:25:48,640 --> 00:25:52,200
Fifteen years later, Totten's statement would be put to the test.

383
00:25:54,960 --> 00:26:04,580
In December of 1860, federal troops took over Fort Sumter in Charleston, South Carolina, and the war between the states began.

384
00:26:04,581 --> 00:26:14,100
In January of 1861, the Confederate States of America seized Fort Pulaski and began arming it for battle.

385
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:20,220
In the meantime, the Union Army, led by Engineer Captain Quincy A. Gilmore,

386
00:26:20,680 --> 00:26:26,520
was building a series of field fortifications and artillery batteries on Tybee Island,

387
00:26:27,140 --> 00:26:29,380
a mile away across the Savannah River.

388
00:26:29,381 --> 00:26:43,381
By April of 1862, they had painstakingly constructed 11 artillery batteries containing 36 cannons and mortars, all aimed directly at Fort Pulaski.

389
00:26:45,800 --> 00:26:55,460
Gilmore decided to try out a new type of unproven rifled artillery for the assault, and that proved to be the death knell for Fort Pulaski.

390
00:26:55,461 --> 00:27:01,460
Well, the point in history or the technology is a new type of cannon

391
00:27:01,461 --> 00:27:07,100
that would make this fort and all others all over the world obsolete.

392
00:27:07,720 --> 00:27:12,400
On April 10th, 1862, Gilmore launched his assault.

393
00:27:13,840 --> 00:27:18,820
Soldiers on both sides were surprised by the performance of this new rifled cannonball.

394
00:27:19,460 --> 00:27:23,240
It could hit targets precisely over a mile away.

395
00:27:23,241 --> 00:27:31,240
The ribs in the projectile allowed the blast to propel it on a faster, longer and more direct trajectory.

396
00:27:34,240 --> 00:27:41,480
The Union fired over 5,000 shots in about 30 hours before Colonel Charles Olmsted surrendered.

397
00:27:43,680 --> 00:27:49,700
The precision of the cannon fire had succeeded in breaching two walls of Fort Pulaski

398
00:27:49,701 --> 00:27:52,820
and damaged the door to the powder magazine

399
00:27:52,821 --> 00:27:56,260
where 40,000 pounds of gunpowder was stored.

400
00:27:57,120 --> 00:28:00,580
When Olmstead found the magazine filling with smoke,

401
00:28:00,900 --> 00:28:03,120
he knew that the thing to do would be to surrender

402
00:28:03,121 --> 00:28:06,460
because it would have just been a matter of time

403
00:28:06,461 --> 00:28:08,540
before the magazine would have detonated.

404
00:28:09,040 --> 00:28:10,500
That much powder going off,

405
00:28:10,580 --> 00:28:13,320
the concussion probably would have killed most of the men in here

406
00:28:13,321 --> 00:28:16,140
without ever having a chance to try to defend themselves.

407
00:28:16,141 --> 00:28:22,140
After only 30 hours, Colonel Olmstead surrendered Fort Pulaski.

408
00:28:22,141 --> 00:28:26,140
After the battle was over, only 14 people were injured in the fort.

409
00:28:26,141 --> 00:28:29,140
One would die at the time and one apparently later on.

410
00:28:29,141 --> 00:28:33,140
Over on Tybee, there was not one single person wounded and only one man killed.

411
00:28:33,141 --> 00:28:37,140
So for a Civil War battle, it was relatively bloodless.

412
00:28:37,141 --> 00:28:45,140
The effect of the new rifled artillery would change military engineering forever.

413
00:28:45,940 --> 00:28:52,700
The first black Union Army Regiment was comprised of the slaves freed at Fort Pulaski.

414
00:28:52,701 --> 00:28:59,100
Modern marvels will return in a moment.

415
00:28:59,101 --> 00:29:04,060
In the early 1800s, while the United States government was building up its coastal forts

416
00:29:04,061 --> 00:29:13,300
on the Atlantic seaboard, the settling of the Western Territory was also gaining momentum.

417
00:29:13,301 --> 00:29:18,100
In the wilderness of Wyoming, beaver trappers and fur traders were playing a crucial role

418
00:29:18,101 --> 00:29:20,540
in this expansion.

419
00:29:20,541 --> 00:29:26,260
The first fort in the area was established as a meeting place for Indians, trappers,

420
00:29:26,261 --> 00:29:30,880
and those who wanted to purchase the pelts.

421
00:29:30,881 --> 00:29:35,960
It was situated on the Laramie River, near its confluence with the Platte.

422
00:29:35,961 --> 00:29:42,920
The fort was built throughout the summer of 1834, and it was a classic log palisade fort,

423
00:29:42,921 --> 00:29:45,560
Walls were maybe 15, 16 feet high.

424
00:29:45,680 --> 00:29:47,760
It may have been around 100 feet square.

425
00:29:48,140 --> 00:29:51,020
It had block houses on its corners and above its main gate.

426
00:29:51,880 --> 00:29:56,060
An image of it was painted in 1837 by a traveling artist named Alfred Jacob Miller.

427
00:29:56,600 --> 00:30:01,040
And ever since, it has become a quintessential image of a Western frontier fort.

428
00:30:01,660 --> 00:30:07,480
When the beaver fur trade became depressed, the entrepreneurs turned to buffalo.

429
00:30:07,481 --> 00:30:10,480
The Indians were happy to trade their abundant hides

430
00:30:10,481 --> 00:30:14,480
for the goods available at the fort.

431
00:30:14,481 --> 00:30:19,480
The primary purpose of the trader fort's walls was to prevent theft.

432
00:30:19,481 --> 00:30:24,480
They were loaded with highly valuable trade goods.

433
00:30:24,481 --> 00:30:30,480
Guns, knives, axes, blankets, cloth, cooking pots,

434
00:30:30,481 --> 00:30:32,480
all kinds of things that, to the plains tribes,

435
00:30:32,481 --> 00:30:39,480
tribes were enormous sources of wealth.

436
00:30:39,580 --> 00:30:43,020
The Buffalo hide business proved to be promising.

437
00:30:43,120 --> 00:30:46,920
And in 1836, the American Fur Company bought the fort

438
00:30:47,020 --> 00:30:50,040
and replaced the wooden walls with adobe.

439
00:30:50,140 --> 00:30:52,240
And it was a very, very big business.

440
00:30:52,340 --> 00:30:55,600
And like business today, image is very important.

441
00:30:55,700 --> 00:30:57,380
Image is prestige.

442
00:30:57,480 --> 00:31:00,480
And the forts that the fur traders built often

443
00:31:00,481 --> 00:31:03,720
were built not necessarily for defense,

444
00:31:03,721 --> 00:31:07,860
but to reflect the prestige and power of the company.

445
00:31:07,861 --> 00:31:10,920
And by the 1830s, the American Fur Company

446
00:31:10,921 --> 00:31:15,100
had become America's richest corporation.

447
00:31:15,101 --> 00:31:18,460
In the 1840s, the number of emigrants to the west

448
00:31:18,461 --> 00:31:20,800
began to increase.

449
00:31:20,801 --> 00:31:23,440
In 1849, the United States government

450
00:31:23,441 --> 00:31:26,780
purchased the fort and made it a military outpost

451
00:31:26,781 --> 00:31:29,720
to protect the pioneers along the Oregon Trail.

452
00:31:30,980 --> 00:31:33,240
They were going to California to get rich.

453
00:31:33,360 --> 00:31:36,720
They were going to Oregon to find farmland.

454
00:31:37,020 --> 00:31:40,140
They were going to Utah for religious freedom.

455
00:31:40,640 --> 00:31:43,400
They weren't planning to stay out here on the plains.

456
00:31:43,820 --> 00:31:49,360
And so the Army's role was not to help settle these plains,

457
00:31:49,361 --> 00:31:53,240
but merely to facilitate travel and communication

458
00:31:53,241 --> 00:32:00,020
and keep the Indians at bay for the duration of that mission.

459
00:32:01,040 --> 00:32:06,580
In 1851, Fort Laramie played host to the Indian leader Red Cloud

460
00:32:06,581 --> 00:32:11,640
and a tribal treaty council to promote peace within the warring Indian factions.

461
00:32:13,320 --> 00:32:17,980
The army never rebuilt the decaying adobe walls around Fort Laramie,

462
00:32:18,300 --> 00:32:21,320
as they did not feel a direct threat from the Indians.

463
00:32:23,240 --> 00:32:29,620
Well, having Indians besiege a fort is something that we see a lot in the movies,

464
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:32,780
but it seldom happened in reality.

465
00:32:34,280 --> 00:32:42,480
Plains Indians usually tried to avoid the army and its forts as much as possible.

466
00:32:43,140 --> 00:32:48,200
But there was one incident when a small group of soldiers came back from patrol

467
00:32:48,201 --> 00:32:53,280
and allowed their horses a much-needed rest on the open area in the middle of the fort.

468
00:32:53,980 --> 00:32:59,880
So the cavalry horses, about 30 head, had just no sooner been unsaddled

469
00:32:59,881 --> 00:33:06,600
and were turned loose on the parade ground when a Sioux war party swept through the post,

470
00:33:07,500 --> 00:33:11,740
scooped up the horses, went right out the other side, and disappeared.

471
00:33:13,300 --> 00:33:17,880
That's bravery, and one has to admire the audacity.

472
00:33:18,200 --> 00:33:20,400
of an act like that.

473
00:33:20,401 --> 00:33:23,100
Life at the fort was relatively comfortable.

474
00:33:25,800 --> 00:33:30,140
Most of the soldiers belonged to cavalry and infantry units.

475
00:33:30,141 --> 00:33:33,720
And at its peak, there were over 700 troops stationed

476
00:33:33,721 --> 00:33:34,421
at the post.

477
00:33:39,520 --> 00:33:42,260
The fort had communal barracks for the enlisted men.

478
00:33:42,261 --> 00:33:48,100
and the officers had separate quarters.

479
00:33:49,720 --> 00:33:52,220
Fort Laramie still acted as a trading post

480
00:33:52,221 --> 00:33:56,600
and was a major station for the Pony Express in 1860.

481
00:33:57,640 --> 00:33:59,200
The Pony Express didn't last long.

482
00:33:59,320 --> 00:34:00,660
It lasted only a very short time

483
00:34:00,661 --> 00:34:02,400
because it was supplanted then by another,

484
00:34:02,540 --> 00:34:04,260
even more effective means of communication,

485
00:34:04,600 --> 00:34:06,560
the transcontinental telegraph.

486
00:34:07,080 --> 00:34:09,100
By the late 1850s,

487
00:34:09,101 --> 00:34:14,540
over 50,000 emigrants visited Fort Laramie each year.

488
00:34:14,541 --> 00:34:17,940
Many of those visitors were prominent people of their time.

489
00:34:17,941 --> 00:34:20,880
One of them was Mark Twain, and another was Wyatt Earp.

490
00:34:20,881 --> 00:34:25,480
Great Indian chiefs, Red Cloud, Crazy Horse, Spotted Tail,

491
00:34:25,481 --> 00:34:27,820
great army generals, and a lot of people

492
00:34:27,821 --> 00:34:29,860
that we've never heard of, of course.

493
00:34:29,861 --> 00:34:32,220
Nameless, faceless peoples, people

494
00:34:32,221 --> 00:34:35,100
that made up the real story of American westward expansion.

495
00:34:39,100 --> 00:34:47,240
When the mass emigration West slowed down in the 1870s, Fort Laramie no longer served

496
00:34:47,241 --> 00:34:48,600
a vital purpose.

497
00:34:48,601 --> 00:35:00,200
General Sherman had expressed his desire as early as 1880, 1882, that the army should

498
00:35:00,201 --> 00:35:08,880
consolidate its forts and abandon those that were not found necessary.

499
00:35:08,881 --> 00:35:12,880
Fort Laramie officially closed in 1890.

500
00:35:12,881 --> 00:35:14,880
It was born of the frontier.

501
00:35:14,881 --> 00:35:16,880
It made that frontier happen.

502
00:35:16,881 --> 00:35:20,880
When the frontier closed, so did Fort Laramie.

503
00:35:23,880 --> 00:35:26,880
Less than 30 years later, in the early 1900s,

504
00:35:26,881 --> 00:35:29,880
the United States Armed Forces turned their attention

505
00:35:29,881 --> 00:35:32,880
to the Great War in Europe.

506
00:35:32,881 --> 00:35:35,880
There were no fixed fortifications.

507
00:35:35,881 --> 00:35:42,880
Soldiers on the front lines launched offensives from trenches and underground bunkers.

508
00:35:42,881 --> 00:35:49,880
This new aggressive form of warfare proved devastating, especially to the French.

509
00:35:49,881 --> 00:35:52,880
They lost over a million men in the Great War,

510
00:35:52,881 --> 00:35:57,880
and over four million more were wounded or taken prisoner.

511
00:35:57,881 --> 00:36:02,220
In 1920, Andre Maginot, the French Minister of War,

512
00:36:02,380 --> 00:36:05,680
secured financing for a Great Wall of France

513
00:36:05,681 --> 00:36:08,560
to discourage further attack by Germany.

514
00:36:10,720 --> 00:36:13,800
It lined the border between France and Germany

515
00:36:13,801 --> 00:36:17,640
and stretched from the Rhine River all the way to Luxembourg.

516
00:36:19,320 --> 00:36:23,280
Maginot's plan was an ultra-modern defensive system for its time

517
00:36:23,281 --> 00:36:25,540
because except for the gun turrets,

518
00:36:25,541 --> 00:36:28,540
the majority of the forts were built underground,

519
00:36:29,040 --> 00:36:34,220
sometimes as deep as 100 feet to protect the troops from a turned fire bombardment.

520
00:36:37,960 --> 00:36:43,260
The 58 large forts and 400 smaller ones were connected by tunnels

521
00:36:43,261 --> 00:36:46,720
that hauled equipment and ammunition between the blocks.

522
00:36:47,600 --> 00:36:51,680
Each fort had 10-foot-thick walls made of reinforced concrete.

523
00:36:51,681 --> 00:36:55,680
Here, over a thousand men would eat, sleep, train,

524
00:36:55,681 --> 00:37:00,680
and wait months for a confrontation that never came.

525
00:37:00,681 --> 00:37:04,680
They even had a special system of air filtering.

526
00:37:04,681 --> 00:37:07,680
The air inside these blocks

527
00:37:07,681 --> 00:37:10,680
and inside the entire marginal line forts

528
00:37:10,681 --> 00:37:14,680
was kept at a higher pressure than the outside.

529
00:37:14,681 --> 00:37:17,680
And any air that was taken into the forts was filtered.

530
00:37:17,681 --> 00:37:20,680
The object was to keep any poison gas out

531
00:37:20,681 --> 00:37:25,360
gas out because poison gas had been such a major issue during the First World War.

532
00:37:26,140 --> 00:37:32,520
Despite the supposed impregnable nature of these fortifications, they were never really tested.

533
00:37:33,240 --> 00:37:39,160
In 1940, Hitler simply chose to ignore the Great Wall and attack the Ardennes in Belgium

534
00:37:39,161 --> 00:37:42,500
to completely surround the French army in Alsace.

535
00:37:43,420 --> 00:37:48,840
Less than one month later, the proud fortress soldiers marched into captivity.

536
00:37:50,680 --> 00:37:54,000
The Maginot Line, in some respects, did its job.

537
00:37:54,320 --> 00:37:56,940
The Germans did not come through it in any significant way.

538
00:37:57,000 --> 00:37:57,720
They went round it.

539
00:37:58,380 --> 00:38:01,720
Unfortunately, the French weren't very well prepared for this,

540
00:38:01,860 --> 00:38:03,740
and we all know what happened.

541
00:38:03,741 --> 00:38:07,780
But the actual forts of the Maginot Line held out fairly well.

542
00:38:08,620 --> 00:38:13,420
The Maginot Line dissuaded the Germans from attacking France's eastern frontier,

543
00:38:13,760 --> 00:38:17,840
and it gave the French army time to fully mobilize and deploy.

544
00:38:17,841 --> 00:38:31,560
But its legacy was the lesson that modern wars were mobile affairs, battles that were won or lost by the strength of artillery, or tanks, or aircraft power.

545
00:38:32,720 --> 00:38:36,380
Failure of the Maginot Line caused General Patton to comment,

546
00:38:37,140 --> 00:38:41,100
Permanent fortification is a monument to man's stupidity.

547
00:38:41,101 --> 00:38:48,660
In the 1970s, many of the Maginot Line forts were auctioned off to the public.

548
00:38:49,360 --> 00:38:53,280
Some were open for tourists, and others were used for mushroom farming.

549
00:38:54,160 --> 00:38:56,220
Modern marvels will return in a moment.

550
00:38:59,480 --> 00:39:08,800
After World War II, the threat of nuclear annihilation haunted the minds of military engineers and defense strategists around the world.

551
00:39:11,100 --> 00:39:14,000
Permanent fortifications were no longer viable,

552
00:39:14,001 --> 00:39:17,000
considering the power of a detonated missile.

553
00:39:18,540 --> 00:39:22,080
Early detection and quick response time became the goal.

554
00:39:26,240 --> 00:39:30,260
In 1966, the North American Air Defense Command Center

555
00:39:30,261 --> 00:39:33,060
opened its 30,000 pound doors.

556
00:39:35,020 --> 00:39:37,600
It was built under 2,000 feet of granite

557
00:39:37,601 --> 00:39:39,900
in Cheyenne Mountain in Colorado.

558
00:39:39,901 --> 00:39:43,900
A fort for the nuclear age.

559
00:39:43,901 --> 00:39:46,900
NORAD is basically an electronic sentinel

560
00:39:46,901 --> 00:39:48,900
for allied forces around the world.

561
00:39:48,901 --> 00:39:53,900
It was here that the Scud missiles launched by Iraq were identified,

562
00:39:53,901 --> 00:39:56,900
and anti-ballistic missiles were alerted to respond.

563
00:39:56,901 --> 00:40:02,900
...a launch and impact of a missile out of southern Iraq.

564
00:40:02,901 --> 00:40:05,900
Predicted impact in Saudi Arabia.

565
00:40:05,901 --> 00:40:14,540
1400 men and women work in this four and a half acre complex that is a maze of chambers and tunnels.

566
00:40:15,660 --> 00:40:20,300
The center itself is crafted to withstand a medium range nuclear blast

567
00:40:20,301 --> 00:40:24,700
and its inhabitants would be able to survive for up to 30 days in that event.

568
00:40:28,700 --> 00:40:34,620
At the same time that NORAD was being built, Air Force and aerospace engineers were developing a

569
00:40:34,621 --> 00:40:38,620
a smaller nuclear missile that would be lighter in weight,

570
00:40:38,621 --> 00:40:41,620
easier to launch, but still be effective.

571
00:40:42,620 --> 00:40:45,620
When those two technologies merged,

572
00:40:45,621 --> 00:40:47,620
the capability to develop a strategic weapon

573
00:40:47,621 --> 00:40:50,620
to be able to project a deterrent force

574
00:40:50,621 --> 00:40:55,620
over 5,000 miles distance without any personnel with the weapon

575
00:40:55,621 --> 00:40:59,620
became a very useful tool in a strategic perspective.

576
00:40:59,621 --> 00:41:01,620
Since the 1960s,

577
00:41:01,621 --> 00:41:05,220
these intercontinental ballistic missile launch systems

578
00:41:05,221 --> 00:41:08,560
have been fashioned into many fortresses themselves.

579
00:41:10,000 --> 00:41:12,660
They've been housed underground at complexes

580
00:41:12,661 --> 00:41:16,180
like the Vandenberg Air Force Base in Lompoc, California.

581
00:41:17,820 --> 00:41:19,520
A system that is self-sustainable.

582
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:23,540
It is a defensive system on its own.

583
00:41:23,541 --> 00:41:27,160
It has its own crew quarters for its personnel

584
00:41:27,161 --> 00:41:30,320
and has its own power generation capability,

585
00:41:30,321 --> 00:41:37,420
its own atmospheric controls, and it is housing a weapon system that, if used, is a very strategic,

586
00:41:37,840 --> 00:41:39,040
strategic weapon.

587
00:41:41,780 --> 00:41:48,180
The Minuteman, Titan II, and Peacekeeper missiles weigh approximately 165 tons.

588
00:41:49,260 --> 00:41:52,920
The silos where they are kept are roughly 12 stories underground.

589
00:41:54,440 --> 00:41:59,000
The door that covers that silo weighs 748 tons,

590
00:41:59,001 --> 00:42:02,040
And that door opens in 19 seconds.

591
00:42:02,041 --> 00:42:04,460
Now that facility is connected to a launch control center.

592
00:42:04,461 --> 00:42:08,360
And the launch control center would be a three-story structure underground.

593
00:42:08,361 --> 00:42:12,580
Should an explosive device be detonated near the facility, everything required to operate

594
00:42:12,581 --> 00:42:17,580
the weapon system is hanging on shock isolation systems, or coil springs in the case of the

595
00:42:17,581 --> 00:42:19,240
Titan II program.

596
00:42:19,241 --> 00:42:25,300
The facility is designed to be able to withstand, essentially, any attack that would be thrown

597
00:42:25,301 --> 00:42:26,620
against it.

598
00:42:26,621 --> 00:42:30,620
So as the aggressor comes to try and take the control of the silo,

599
00:42:30,621 --> 00:42:34,900
be it through an attack via a strategic missile,

600
00:42:34,901 --> 00:42:37,400
strategic missile versus strategic missile,

601
00:42:37,401 --> 00:42:39,260
the system is designed to be able to withstand

602
00:42:39,261 --> 00:42:41,600
the effects of those attacks.

603
00:42:41,601 --> 00:42:45,940
If deployed, the missiles travel at a speed of up to 17,000

604
00:42:45,941 --> 00:42:46,940
miles per hour.

605
00:42:50,240 --> 00:42:55,180
They have a pinpoint accuracy of roughly 100 meters.

606
00:42:55,181 --> 00:42:57,040
like throwing a baseball.

607
00:42:57,041 --> 00:42:59,380
The accuracy of the baseball toss

608
00:42:59,381 --> 00:43:01,660
is dependent on the speed and angle

609
00:43:01,661 --> 00:43:04,260
at which you release the ball.

610
00:43:04,261 --> 00:43:07,660
The missile silo is to some the heir apparent

611
00:43:07,661 --> 00:43:11,820
to the age-old notion of the impregnable fortress.

612
00:43:11,821 --> 00:43:14,740
The original fortress was designed

613
00:43:14,741 --> 00:43:18,900
to protect its contents, be that an operational

614
00:43:18,901 --> 00:43:22,280
or defensive position or asset.

615
00:43:22,281 --> 00:43:25,740
the ICBM, is in a sense both.

616
00:43:25,741 --> 00:43:27,380
It has its own defensive posture.

617
00:43:27,381 --> 00:43:30,120
It is an offensive device, but it is defensive in the nature

618
00:43:30,121 --> 00:43:33,840
that it is a deterrent system.

619
00:43:33,841 --> 00:43:36,640
Systems of defense and attack have evolved

620
00:43:36,641 --> 00:43:38,380
throughout history.

621
00:43:38,381 --> 00:43:40,480
But while the tactics have changed,

622
00:43:40,481 --> 00:43:42,640
the principles are the same.

623
00:43:42,641 --> 00:43:44,540
The forts of today may or may not

624
00:43:44,541 --> 00:43:47,960
become tourist attractions when they're left behind.

625
00:43:47,961 --> 00:43:51,700
Perhaps they will be named after heroic generals.

626
00:43:51,701 --> 00:43:58,320
But they are efficient and effective, a threat and a deterrent.

627
00:43:58,321 --> 00:44:01,260
And that is the point of any fortification.

